'''Daniel James McKee''' (born June 16, 1951) is an American politician and businessman serving since 2021 as the 76th [[governor of Rhode Island]]. A member of the [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic Party]], he served from 2015 to 2021 as Rhode Island's 69th [[Lieutenant Governor of Rhode Island|lieutenant governor]].

Born in [[Cumberland, Rhode Island]], McKee received his undergraduate degree from [[Assumption University (Worcester)|Assumption College]] and received his master's degree from the [[Harvard Kennedy School]]. He served on the [[Cumberland, Rhode Island|Cumberland]] [[town council]] from 1992 to 1998 and as mayor of Cumberland twice, from 2000 to 2004 and from 2006 until 2014.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.providencejournal.com/article/20131113/NEWS/311139978|title=Cumberland Mayor McKee announces bid for R.I. lieutenant governor|last=Edgar|first=Randal|website=Providence Journal|language=en|access-date=November 21, 2019}}</ref> McKee was elected lieutenant governor in 2014 and reelected in 2018. When Governor [[Gina Raimondo]] resigned upon being confirmed as [[United States Secretary of Commerce]] in 2021, McKee ascended to the governorship.<ref>{{Cite web|author1=Patrick Anderson|author2=Katherine Gregg |title=Raimondo resigns after winning Senate confirmation as U.S. commerce secretary; McKee sworn in as RI governor|url=https://www.providencejournal.com/story/news/politics/2021/03/02/ri-may-have-new-governor-today-heres-how-would-happen/6883302002/|access-date=2021-03-12|website=The Providence Journal|language=en-US}}</ref> He was elected to a full term [[2022 Rhode Island gubernatorial election|in 2022]]. He ran for a second full term in [[2026 Rhode Island gubernatorial election|2026]], but lost renomination to [[Helena Foulkes]].<ref>{{Cite web| title= Helena Foulkes wins Democratic nomination for governor in Rhode Island| url= https://turnto10.com/news/local/mckee-and-foulkes-cast-their-ballots-as-rhode-island-heads-to-the-polls-sept-10-2026| access-date= September 9, 2026| website= WJAR}}</ref>

==Early life and education==
After graduating from [[Cumberland High School (Rhode Island)|Cumberland High School]], McKee received a Bachelor of Arts in education and political science from [[Assumption College (Worcester)|Assumption College]] in [[Worcester, Massachusetts]], in 1973. He earned a [[Master of Public Administration]] from the [[Harvard Kennedy School]] in 2005.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://justfacts.votesmart.org/candidate/biography/80328/dan-mckee|title=Dan McKee's Biography|website=[[Vote Smart]]|access-date=September 26, 2022}}</ref>

==Early career==
McKee was an officer of McKee Brothers, a heating, air conditioning, and home heating oil delivery business his grandfather founded.<ref name="Randal">{{cite news |last=Edgar |first=Randal |date=August 24, 2014 |title=Democrats in R.I. lieutenant governor race put economy first |url=https://www.providencejournal.com/article/20140824/News/308249995?template=ampart |work=[[The Providence Journal]] |location=Providence, RI}}</ref> He also ran a health and fitness business for more than 30 years.<ref name="Randal"/>

During his time on the [[Cumberland, Rhode Island]] Town Council (1992–1998), McKee was also a basketball coach.{{cn|date=November 2024}}

McKee served six terms as Cumberland's mayor.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Governor Dan McKee {{!}} Governor's Office, State of Rhode Island |url=https://governor.ri.gov/about |access-date=2024-11-19 |website=governor.ri.gov}}</ref>

McKee has been a member of the board of directors of the [[Boys & Girls Clubs of America|Boys and Girls Club]] of Cumberland-Lincoln for over 25 years, serving as past president of the executive board and chair of the endowment committee.{{cn|date=November 2024}}

==Lieutenant governor of Rhode Island (2015-2021)==
In 2013, McKee announced his candidacy for lieutenant governor of Rhode Island. He defeated [[Secretary of State of Rhode Island]] [[Ralph Mollis]] and State Representative [[Frank Ferri]] in the [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic]] primary.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://ballotpedia.org/Rhode_Island_lieutenant_gubernatorial_election,_2014|title=Rhode Island lieutenant gubernatorial election, 2014|website=Ballotpedia|language=en|access-date=November 21, 2019}}</ref> In the general election, he defeated [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] nominee Catherine Terry Taylor, a legislative aide and speechwriter for U.S. senators [[John Chafee]] and [[Lincoln Chafee]],<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.golocalprov.com/lifestyle/firstworks-names-taylor-president-of-board|title=FirstWorks Names Taylor President of Board|author=ChrisS|website=GoLocalProv|language=en|access-date=November 21, 2019}}</ref> with 54.3% of the vote. He was reelected in 2018.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.wpri.com/news/lt-gov-mckee-wins-re-election/|title=Lt. Gov. McKee wins re-election|date=November 7, 2018|website=WPRI|language=en-US|access-date=November 21, 2019}}</ref>

On January 7, 2021, President-elect [[Joe Biden]] selected then-Rhode Island governor [[Gina Raimondo]] as [[United States Secretary of Commerce|Secretary of Commerce]]. Since McKee was next in line of succession, he became governor once Raimondo was confirmed by the [[United States Senate]] on March 2, 2021.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.npr.org/2021/03/02/962856664/bidens-commerce-secretary-gina-raimondo-confirmed-by-senate|title=Biden's Commerce Secretary, Gina Raimondo, Confirmed By Senate|website=NPR.org|date=March 2, 2021 |accessdate=March 3, 2021|last1=Wang |first1=Hansi Lo }}</ref>

In February 2021, McKee began to form a [[COVID-19]] advisory board.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bostonglobe.com/2021/02/03/metro/mckee-names-covid-19-advisory-group/|title=McKee names COVID advisory board|publisher=Boston Globe|date=February 3, 2021}}</ref> He had criticized the Raimondo administration over a slow COVID-19 vaccine rollout.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.wpri.com/news/politics-government/mckee-criticizes-raimondo-administration-over-vaccine-rollout/|title=McKee criticizes Raimondo administration over vaccine rollout|date=February 15, 2021 |publisher=WPRI|accessdate=3 March 2021}}</ref>

==Governor of Rhode Island (2021-present<!--2027-->)==
[[File:Sabina_Matos_and_Dan_McKee.jpg|thumb|McKee with Lt. Gov. [[Sabina Matos]] in 2023]]
McKee was sworn in as the 76th [[governor of Rhode Island]] on March 2, 2021.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.providencejournal.com/story/news/politics/2021/03/02/ri-may-have-new-governor-today-heres-how-would-happen/6883302002/|title=Raimondo resigns after winning Senate confirmation as U.S. commerce secretary; McKee sworn in as RI governor|first1=Katherine|last1=Gregg|first2=Patrick|last2=Anderson|website=The Providence Journal|accessdate=March 3, 2021}}</ref>

In 2021, during the [[COVID-19 pandemic in Rhode Island]], McKee said his main priority was to advance [[COVID-19 vaccine]] rollout and contain the outbreak.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.necn.com/news/local/ri-lt-gov-says-speeding-up-vaccine-distribution-is-his-top-priority/2406883/|title=RI Lt. Gov. Says Speeding Up Vaccine Distribution Is His 'Top Priority'|date=February 16, 2021 |publisher=NECN|accessdate=March 3, 2021}}</ref> By July 4, Rhode Island had fully vaccinated over 633,000 people, 70% of its eligible adult population.<ref name="MassLive20210703">{{cite news |title=Rhode Island reaches 70% COVID vaccination rate |url=https://www.masslive.com/coronavirus/2021/07/rhode-island-reaches-70-covid-vaccination-rate.html |access-date=10 July 2021 |agency=Associated Press |publisher=MassLive |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210710140933/https://www.masslive.com/coronavirus/2021/07/rhode-island-reaches-70-covid-vaccination-rate.html |archive-date=10 July 2021|date=3 July 2021}}</ref> It was the fifth state to reach that milestone.<ref name="MassLive20210703" /> Also in July, McKee terminated the state mask mandate, but extended the COVID-19 emergency declaration to August 6, citing the prevalence of the highly transmissible [[SARS-CoV-2 Delta variant|Delta variant]].<ref name="ProJo20210709">{{cite news |last1=Anderson |first1=Patrick |title=Governor McKee extends emergency, 3 additional cases of delta variant in RI |url=https://www.providencejournal.com/story/news/politics/2021/07/09/mckee-extends-emergency-order-ri-additional-cases-delta-variant/7906488002/ |access-date=10 July 2021 |publisher=The Providence Journal |date=9 July 2021}}</ref> 
On February 8, 2022, McKee announced that Rhode Island would lift [[Face masks during the COVID-19 pandemic in the United States|mask mandates]] as the infection rate fell.{{cn|date=November 2024}}

In September 2021, McKee signed legislation that reclassified simple possession of 10 grams or less of certain controlled substances{{Which|date=June 2025}} as a misdemeanor rather than a felony. He signed the legislation at [[Project Weber/RENEW]]'s office.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2021-09-28 |title=Governor McKee signs legislation reclassifying certain drug possession charges |url=https://www.abc6.com/governor-mckee-signs-legislation-reclassifying-certain-drug-possession-charges/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210928193323/https://www.abc6.com/governor-mckee-signs-legislation-reclassifying-certain-drug-possession-charges/ |url-status=dead |archive-date=September 28, 2021 |access-date=2023-02-07 |website=ABC6 |language=en-US}}</ref>

On February 22, 2022, McKee announced that he was running for reelection to a full four-year term.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2022-02-22 |title=Gov. McKee launches campaign for a full term, flanked by mayors |url=https://www.wpri.com/news/politics/gov-mckee-to-launch-campaign-for-a-full-term-flanked-by-mayors/ |access-date=2022-09-14 |website=WPRI.com |language=en-US}}</ref> He won the September 13 Democratic primary, defeating four challengers in a close race. He defeated Republican nominee Ashley Kalus in the [[2022 Rhode Island gubernatorial election|general election]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Ulloa |first=Jazmine |date=2022-09-14 |title=McKee, Rhode Island's Appointed Governor, Wins Democratic Nomination to Run for Seat |language=en-US |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2022/09/13/us/politics/governor-rhode-island-primary-mckee.html |access-date=2022-09-14 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref>

As governor, McKee's approval rating has continuously dropped, and he is now one of the nation's least popular governors,<ref>{{Cite web |last=Lavin |first=Nancy |date=2025-07-09 |title=Bad polling continues to dog McKee, putting pressure on his Washington Bridge rebuild plan |url=https://rhodeislandcurrent.com/2025/07/09/bad-polling-continues-to-dog-mckee-putting-pressure-on-his-washington-bridge-rebuild-plan/ |access-date=2025-08-03 |website=Rhode Island Current |language=en-US}}</ref> largely because of the longtime closure of the [[Washington Bridge (Providence, Rhode Island)|Washington Bridge]].<ref name="politico-20260808">{{Cite web |last1=Okun |first1=Eli |date=August 8, 2026 |title=Why Rhode Island may oust a sitting governor over a bridge |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2026/08/08/rhode-island-governor-bridge-01030024 |access-date=2026-08-12 |website=POLITICO |language=en}}</ref>

McKee supports [[gun control]] and has said he would support an assault weapons ban.<ref name="weaponsban">{{cite web |title=Assault weapons ban, safe storage bills held for further study by R.I. Judiciary Committee |url=https://www.browndailyherald.com/article/2023/04/assault-weapons-ban-safe-storage-bills-held-for-further-study-by-r-i-judiciary-committee |access-date=18 April 2023 |ref=45}}</ref>

